  ('cloud-air-pollution-respiratory-analysis', 'Cloud-Native Air Pollution & Respiratory Death Analysis', 'A cloud data project analysing average PM2.5 pollution and respiratory deaths using Azure and PySpark.', 'Data / Cloud', array['Azure', 'PySpark', 'Big Data', 'Analytics', 'Azure Data Lake Gen2', 'Synapse Spark', 'Plotly']::text[], '/img/projects/cloud-air-pollution-respiratory-analysis.webp', '{}'::text[], '### Project Description

This project analyses the relationship between air pollution exposure and respiratory mortality using a cloud-native data processing workflow. It is designed as a public-health analytics system where pollution and mortality datasets are ingested, cleaned, transformed, modelled and visualised through dashboards. WHO identifies ambient air pollution as a major global health issue and estimates millions of premature deaths linked to outdoor air pollution, making this a strong real-world data project.

### Problem

Environmental and health datasets are often large, distributed and difficult to interpret. Public-health decision makers need dashboards and analytical pipelines that can connect air quality indicators with respiratory death trends.

### Solution

The project proposes a cloud-native analytics architecture using data lake storage, scalable Spark processing, transformed analytical datasets and interactive dashboards. Microsoft Azure Data Lake and Azure Synapse Spark are suitable for this type of scalable cloud data processing.

### Main Features

FeatureDescription

Data Ingestion

Imports pollution and mortality datasets

Data Lake Storage

Stores raw and processed datasets

PySpark Processing

Cleans, joins and aggregates data

Analytical Modelling

Compares pollution exposure and health outcomes

Visual Dashboard

Shows trends by location, year and pollutant

Risk Interpretation

Identifies high-risk regions or periods

### Documentation Angle

This project should be documented as a **cloud data engineering and public health analytics case study**. Include architecture diagram, data pipeline, ETL process, dataset table, dashboard screenshots, limitations and ethical use of public health data.', '', '', '', '/docs/cloud-air-pollution-respiratory-analysis.pdf', 'Academic Project', 'Data Engineer', '2026', false, true, 6, null),
  ('pentest-ont-cybersecurity-ontology', 'PenTest-Ont Cybersecurity Ontology', 'A cybersecurity ontology modelling tools, techniques, vulnerabilities and penetration testing knowledge.', 'Cybersecurity', array['OWL', 'Protégé', 'SPARQL', 'Cybersecurity', 'Knowledge Graphs']::text[], '/img/projects/pentest-ont-cybersecurity-ontology.webp', '{}'::text[], '### Project Description

PenTest-Ont Cybersecurity Ontology is a semantic knowledge-modelling project that structures penetration testing concepts, tools, vulnerabilities, attack techniques and mitigation relationships using ontology engineering. OWL 2 is a W3C ontology language for representing classes, properties, individuals and relationships, while SPARQL is used to query RDF/semantic graph data. Protégé is a widely used ontology editor for building and reasoning over OWL ontologies.

### Problem

Cybersecurity knowledge is often fragmented across tools, reports, vulnerability databases, attack techniques and testing methodologies. This makes it difficult to connect what vulnerability exists, which tool detects it, which attack technique exploits it and which mitigation reduces risk.

### Solution

The ontology creates a structured knowledge graph where cybersecurity entities are linked logically. For example, a vulnerability can be connected to an attack category, a testing tool, an affected asset and a mitigation technique.

### Main Features

FeatureDescription

Ontology Classes

Vulnerability, Tool, Attack, Asset, Mitigation

Object Properties

Detects, exploits, affects, mitigates

Reasoning

Infers relationships between concepts

SPARQL Queries

Retrieves meaningful security knowledge

Knowledge Graph

Visual structure of cybersecurity domain

Reporting Support

Can support AI/report automation later

### Documentation Angle

This project should be documented as a **cybersecurity knowledge engineering research project** with ontology design, competency questions, class hierarchy, property table, SPARQL query examples and reasoning results.', '', '', '', '/docs/pentest-ont-cybersecurity-ontology.pdf', 'Academic Project', 'Security Researcher', '2026', false, true, 7, null),
  ('automarketpro-car-marketplace', 'AutoMarketPro Car Marketplace', 'A car-selling marketplace with seller ads, subscriptions, Stripe checkout, image uploads and admin moderation.', 'Web Systems', array['PHP', 'MySQL', 'Stripe', 'Marketplace', 'Admin', 'User Panels']::text[], '/img/projects/automarketpro-car-marketplace.webp', '{}'::text[], '### Project Description

AutoMarketPro is a car-selling marketplace system designed like a lightweight AutoTrader-style platform. The project allows users to register, buy posting packages, publish vehicle ads, upload car images, manage listings and connect with buyers. The system includes monetisation logic using paid ad packages, featured listings and seller-side controls. Stripe Checkout is suitable for handling hosted payment flows, subscriptions and checkout sessions.

### Problem

Small sellers and local dealers need a focused platform where they can list cars professionally without depending only on social media marketplaces. The business also needs a monetisation model based on ad packages, featured ads and commission.

### Solution

The platform supports seller registration, subscription packages, ad publishing, admin moderation, image uploads, vehicle details, featured upgrades and payment integration.

### Main Features

